BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:f3GqiZgjmpmsl8YkzXPdr
SUMMARY:Characterization of Young Active Stars for Identifying the Youngest
	 Exoplanets
DTSTAMP:20260717T062558Z
DTSTART:20260723T190000Z
DESCRIPTION:&#160\;  Speaker: Shih-Yun Tang  Doctoral Candidate  Thesis Def
	ense  Department: Physics &amp\; Astronomy  Location: Brockman Hall for Ph
	ysics 300  The most direct way to understand planet formation is to study 
	planetary systems in their earliest evolutionary stages. As pre-main-seque
	nce solar analogs\, T Tauri stars (TTSs) are captured before and after the
	 epoch of protoplanetary disk dispersal\, marking the boundary at which th
	e main planetary migration occurs. However\, the intense magnetic activity
	 inherent in these young hosts poses severe challenges for current detecti
	on techniques\, such as the transit and radial velocity (RV) methods. Stro
	ng\, localized magnetic fields suppress convective flux across the stellar
	 photosphere\, creating extensive starspots that not only contaminate true
	 planetary signals but also introduce substantial biases when characterizi
	ng the fundamental parameters of the host stars. Without a robust means to
	 disentangle and characterize these activity signatures\, deriving reliabl
	e physical parameters necessary to infer planetary properties and constrai
	n planet-formation models remains challenging.  &#160\;  This dissertation
	 is dedicated to developing a model to simulate the effects of starspots o
	n high-resolution stellar spectra and exploring their resulting impacts on
	 key observational diagnostics\, including RVs\, photometry\, and temperat
	ure-sensitive spectral lines. The thesis is organized into four primary pa
	rts. First\, we analyze a young binary system\, DI Tau\, to demonstrate ho
	w magnetic activity compromises our ability to obtain reliable stellar par
	ameters. Second\, we calibrate and characterize a set of temperature-sensi
	tive lines in the $H$ band to track short-term and seasonal variations in 
	the apparent effective temperature of active TTSs. Third\, we provide a co
	mprehensive description of the development and verification of EXSPLOSYN\,
	 a grid-integrated\, high-resolution spotted spectral synthesis framework.
	 Finally\, we leverage this simulation to forward-model the surface-temper
	ature inhomogeneities of the weak-lined TTS LkCa 4 and found wavelength-de
	pendent agreement between our simulated profiles and empirical datasets\, 
	confirming the reported differences in sensitivity to the hot and cool com
	ponents at different wavelengths. With EXSPLOSYN\, we also provided a crit
	ical benchmark for the widely used two-template spectral-fitting technique
	. We found a potential bias in the technique in overestimating spot size b
	y a factor of 2.  https://riceuniversity.zoom.us/j/92151775597?pwd=o0f7BaK
	C8rAFtoJTmOjaNZxzwEnk7G.1  (Department : Physics &amp\; Astronomy)\n\nhttp
	s://events.rice.edu/event/434278-characterization-of-young-active-stars-fo
	r\n\nFrom Rice University - Open to the Public
URL:https://events.rice.edu/event/434278-characterization-of-young-active-s
	tars-for
GEO:29.7174;-95.4018
LOCATION:Brockman Hall for Physics
X-CALRIPPER-SOURCE:rice-public-events
CATEGORIES:Rice University - Open to the Public
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT1H
END:VEVENT
